WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
What makes the seal between cells selective, and what keeps each cell's two faces

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:11.100
distinct? In chapter one, under gut gradients, carrier placement biased glucose

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.100
toward blood. The seal decided what crossed between cells.

4
00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:23.357
The seal is the tight junction, a belt where neighbouring cells meet near their

5
00:00:23.357 --> 00:00:26.717
apical edges. It is not solid caulk.

6
00:00:27.897 --> 00:00:29.817
It is a selective gate between cells.

7
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:35.070
Claudin proteins assemble into branching strands around each cell.

8
00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.030
Narrow routes through those strands can pass small ions.

9
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:44.650
Meanwhile, the junction strongly restricts bacteria and larger molecules.

10
00:00:45.783 --> 00:00:51.203
The small intestine needs some sodium and water to cross between cells, so its

11
00:00:51.203 --> 00:00:53.263
junctions are relatively leaky.

12
00:00:54.243 --> 00:00:55.963
The bladder needs a tighter barrier.

13
00:00:57.283 --> 00:01:00.083
Different epithelia tune this gate for different jobs.

14
00:01:01.183 --> 00:01:02.823
How can we measure this gate?

15
00:01:04.303 --> 00:01:06.263
Grow cells as a sheet on a porous filter.

16
00:01:07.543 --> 00:01:10.863
Put one electrode above and another below, then pass current.

17
00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:15.063
If resistance falls, must glucose be leaking through?

18
00:01:20.617 --> 00:01:27.257
No. This reading is transepithelial electrical resistance, or TEER.

19
00:01:28.337 --> 00:01:31.217
It reports how easily ions cross the whole sheet.

20
00:01:32.457 --> 00:01:36.537
It cannot name the ion or route, or say whether larger molecules leak.

21
00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:40.767
Current may cross between cells or pass through them.

22
00:01:41.787 --> 00:01:46.107
A low reading can reflect selective ion pores, broader leaks, or breaks.

23
00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:51.647
Telling them apart takes other experiments, set as a design problem below.

24
00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:58.327
The seal is often called a fence, said to stop membrane proteins drifting between

25
00:01:58.327 --> 00:02:03.087
the two faces. How well it fences is tested in the text below.

26
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:09.407
Cells also mark territories with proteins, and traffic delivers cargo to each face.

27
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:15.540
In the standard scheme, Crumbs marks the apical side, while Scribble marks the

28
00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.900
basolateral side. The Par complex sits near their boundary.

29
00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:25.500
These groups are thought to restrain one another, while polarized traffic places

30
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.500
carriers.

31
00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:31.633
Below the seal, an adherens junction couples neighbouring actin belts.

32
00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:35.773
Desmosomes link keratin networks against stretching.

33
00:02:37.273 --> 00:02:40.993
Gap junctions pass ions and small molecules between cytosols.

34
00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:46.147
At the base, hemidesmosomes connect keratin to the basement membrane.

35
00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.207
Each junction has a different job.

36
00:02:50.607 --> 00:02:55.307
Together they let a one-cell-thick sheet stay selective, strong, connected, and

37
00:02:55.307 --> 00:02:56.307
anchored.

38
00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:01.910
Back in the gut, SGLT1 stays apical while the pump stays basolateral.

39
00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:08.190
The selective seal controls the path between cells, while deeper junctions keep the

40
00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:09.190
sheet intact.

41
00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:14.630
That is the rule: epithelial barriers are built by division of labour.

42
00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:21.350
A gate, distinct faces, grips, anchors, and channels work together, but they are not

43
00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:21.890
interchangeable.
